 "response": "Likror's budget is now **$2632K**.\n\n**How I worked it out**\n1. I ignored every message not from the Finance Office (vendor calls, sales updates, PMO notes and so on). I also ignored Finance Office notes that don't change the budget, such as invoice archives and room moves.\n2. I worked out which updates are withdrawn by following each chain of withdrawals to its end. A withdrawal that is itself validly withdrawn stops applying, so its target applies again.\n   - #0013: #0279 cancels #0113, which reinstates #0022. #0022 cancels #0021, which reinstates #0016. #0016 withdraws #0013, so the \u2212$268K does not count.\n   - #0003: #0340 cancels #0332, which reinstates #0168. #0168 cancels #0105, which reinstates #0051. #0051 cancels #0027, so #0003 (\u2212$82K) still applies.\n   - #0607 (+$134K) and #0681 (+$588K) are withdrawn by #1460 and #1448.\n   - #1204 (\u2212$279K) and #1288 (+$203K) are withdrawn by #1453 and #1457.\n3. Starting from the $2090K set in #0001, I applied every remaining increase and decrease in record order.\n\nThis is a long hand calculation over about 1,600 records, so a small slip in the running total is possible.\n\nANSWER: $2632K",
